## Claude Code or Codex cannot reach Bedrock

```bash
# ask each agent from the jupyter container, headless (no first-run dialogs)
jd server exec -- "claude -p 'Reply with OK'"
jd server exec -- "codex exec --skip-git-repo-check 'Reply with OK'"

# the sidecar that publishes the credentials the agents use
jd server status
jd server logs -s bedrock-sidecar
```

- **`jd server status` reports `OUT_OF_SERVICE`**: the `bedrock-sidecar` container is down or
  unhealthy. Read its logs with `jd server logs -s bedrock-sidecar`.
- **`Error when retrieving credentials from custom-process`**: `/run/bedrock-creds/credentials.json`
  is missing; the sidecar has not published credentials yet.
- **`Unable to locate credentials`**: `bedrock_enabled` is `false`. Set it to `true`, then run
  `jd config` and `jd up`.
- **`AccessDeniedException` naming a model or inference profile**: the model is outside
  `bedrock_model_patterns`, or its inference profile is outside `bedrock_inference_scope` (Codex
  outside the US needs `global`).
- **Codex's model reads `unset: ...`**: the deployment gives Codex no model to use; the placeholder
  in `/etc/codex/config.toml` names the variable to change.
- **`AccessDeniedException` on a model the patterns allow**: the model was never enabled in the
  account and region; see below.

## Enable the Bedrock models in a new account

Agents: do not run the commands in this section. They accept a legal agreement on the deployer's
behalf; show them to the user instead.

Bedrock enables a third-party model (Anthropic Claude, OpenAI GPT) on its first invocation in an
account, by subscribing the account through AWS Marketplace. The `bedrock-app` role holds no AWS
Marketplace permissions, so the agents cannot be that first caller: their calls fail with
`AccessDeniedException` until an admin enables the model. This is once per account (per AWS
Organization for the Anthropic form), not per deployment.

Invoking a third-party model, or creating its agreement, accepts the provider's EULA. Review the
terms first.

Run these with a principal that has `aws-marketplace:Subscribe`, `aws-marketplace:ViewSubscriptions`
and `aws-marketplace:Unsubscribe`, in the deployment's region, on an account with a payment method:

1. **Anthropic only: submit the first-time-use form.** Open any Anthropic model in the Bedrock
   console model catalog and submit the use case details, or call
   `aws bedrock put-use-case-for-model-access`.
2. **Enable each model the agents use.** Open the model in the Bedrock console playground
   and send one prompt. Each CLI picks its default model per release: Codex's is the
   `model` line of `/etc/codex/config.toml` in the jupyter container, and `/model` in `claude` lists
   Claude Code's.
   Without the console, create the agreement directly:

   ```bash
   aws bedrock list-foundation-model-agreement-offers --model-id <model-id> --region <region>
   aws bedrock create-foundation-model-agreement --model-id <model-id> --offer-token <offer-token> --region <region>
   ```

3. **Wait for the agreement**, which can take up to 15 minutes; calls fail meanwhile:

   ```bash
   # expect agreementAvailability.status AVAILABLE (PENDING while it is set up)
   aws bedrock get-foundation-model-availability --model-id <model-id> --region <region>
   ```

These `aws bedrock` agreement commands need AWS CLI 2.27.42 or later. An `AccessDeniedException`
that persists once the status is `AVAILABLE` points to an account-level restriction (SCP, or a model
that needs additional access from AWS Sales).
